- **Detetar brute-force local** → Lista em JSON com utilizador/IP e número de tentativas
			-Investigar tipos de brute-force --> Apenas tentativas de password, mesmo
			-Investigar como detectá-los -->

| Sign                                                            | Why It’s Suspicious                                          |
| --------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |
| **High volume of failed logins in a short time**                | Normal users don’t fail dozens/hundreds of times rapidly     |
| **Repeated attempts on the same account**                       | Attackers may target a privileged user or known username     |
| **Attempts using many different usernames**                     | Attackers often guess both usernames and passwords           |
| **Unauthenticated login attempts from the same IP or IP range** | Automation tools often use a single attacking host or subnet |
| **Attempts from foreign / unusual geographic locations**        | Login behavior inconsistent with normal user patterns        |
| **Attempts at unusual hours**                                   | Outside regular working hours = increased suspicion          |
| **Sequential or obvious username patterns**                     | e.g., admin1, admin2, test3 → likely automated enumeration   |
| **Common/weak passwords attempted**                             | "Password1", "123456", etc. = spraying or dictionary attack  |
| **Rapid cycling through different user-agents**                 | Bots switching signatures to evade detection                 |
| **Spike in authentication events compared to baseline**         | Volume anomaly detection can reveal brute-force patterns     |
| **Multiple failures followed by one success**                   | Indicator of cracking an account                             |
| --------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |



